# Resources Exist, but No Means
Tamil title: வளம் இருக்கிறது - வகை இல்லை

Dravida Nadu does not possess only water and fertile land as its natural wealth. It also conceals within itself many forms of mineral wealth in enormous quantities. Yet despite possessing fertile soil, mineral riches and intellectual potential, poverty continues to dance in a land that ought to overflow with wealth, because the people's mental resources have not developed sufficiently.

Dravida Nadu remains an agricultural country, using water that flows, collects and springs in various places to cultivate soil and produce crops. Even this is carried out by methods discovered in ancient times. It has not yet advanced by adopting new methods that reduce time and labour, increase returns, and multiply produce tenfold or a hundredfold.

It remains an ordinary agricultural country rather than developing into an industrial nation. The reason is not a lack of facilities for industrial growth, but a lack of sufficient effort by both people and government.

Abundant raw materials for manufactured goods lie locked beneath the earth. Gold remains buried in the Kolar Gold Fields and within a radius of eighty miles around them.

Iron ore occurs in deposits in Salem district, at Sandur in Bellary district, and in many other places, but has not yet been mined. More than five hundred million tons of coal are reported to lie beneath the stretch from Cuddalore to Vriddhachalam.

Large quantities of uranium, used in atomic research, are hidden at Vaiyampatti village in Tiruchirappalli district and near the Tadagai hills adjoining Travancore. It has been discovered that hundreds of thousands of tons of phosphate bearing phosphorus can be mined in Tiruchirappalli district.

Research conducted in 1946-47 found more than 9.9 million tons of limestone in the Tiruchendur and Nanguneri taluks of Tirunelveli district. Of all regions in the world, the Indian subcontinent contains particularly large quantities of mica, much of it in Dravidian areas. Sulphur-bearing materials too lie buried in many places.

Glass-bearing rock sleeps beneath the ground around Vallam in Thanjavur district. Soil found at Mettupalayam was shown to be suitable for glass, and a glass factory was recently established there. Similar material undoubtedly exists elsewhere beyond our notice.

Earth found at Dalmiapuram in Tiruchirappalli district, Madukkarai in Coimbatore district and a region of Tirunelveli district has been found suitable for cement, and small factories have been established in those places. In Salem district, a kind of clay resembling porcelain lies in masses beneath the earth and appears in small quantities at the surface.

Coimbatore and Tirunelveli districts possess tracts suited to growing good cotton. Proper mills have not yet been created to use all that cotton.

Dravida Nadu has ports suitable for commerce across the world, and dense forests containing timber fit for building ships to anchor in them. If an economical method is developed for separating a petrol-like oil from seawater, seas spreading along three sides are available for obtaining that “water petroleum.”

For producing the large quantities of electricity essential to industry, there are the Sivasamudram falls, Pykara, Mettur Dam and the Papanasam falls.

Despite all this abundance, industry has not developed here. Why?

No easy answer will be obtained while Dravida Nadu remains caught beneath northern domination and authority. As long as it is joined to the North, its natural resources will remain imprisoned as raw materials. Northern capitalism will never accept their conversion into manufactured goods circulating within Dravida Nadu, and the Indian government allied with that capitalism does not desire it either.

Having somehow expanded its industrial strength, the North wishes to supervise the entire Indian subcontinent as a single market of its own. It seeks to prevent industrial growth in any region that might compete with it. If one or two competitors appear, it imposes obstacles and prevents rival factories from arising.

The Indian government, guided by northern industrial rulers, never hesitates to serve their interests. That is why it shows so little concern for industrial growth in the South.

Only very wealthy people or a government can begin major factories. Northern factories have grown gradually under Indian government protection, and wealthy capitalists have consequently emerged there. They now possess the means to raise, invest and withdraw enormous sums.

Industrial development in the South has lagged. If Dravida Nadu attempts to use its natural wealth to establish factories, northern capitalists possess enough money, influence, domination and industrial power to nip them in the bud. When they exercise those powers competitively, no force can restrain them. Provincial governments lack authority to prevent their entry into the South, while the Indian government permits them to circulate throughout the subcontinent, including Dravida Nadu.

Could the Indian government itself expand industry in the South? It could. But because it ventures to act only upon the advice of northern industrial capitalists, its gaze always rests upon the North and never falls upon the South.

Northern capitalism can thus prevent southern industry from developing and, whenever it raises its head even slightly, overpower it through competition.

For example, in 1938-39 a small ironworks was opened at Nagapattinam by Comrade C. Rajagopalachari, then Premier of the Madras Presidency. Within days of its beginning operations, Tata, the northern king of iron, began competing in order to crush it.

On one side stood the Tata company, earning crores of rupees in annual profit; on the other, the Nagapattinam factory begun with only a few lakhs. It was a contest between an elephant and an ant. Iron goods made by Tata were sold far below the prices of the Nagapattinam products. How could an infant factory withstand this?

Unable to meet the competition, its owners appealed to the Madras Premier. Rajagopalachari reportedly replied that the provincial government had no authority to prohibit the Tata company's right to sell in the Presidency. He could offer only one suggestion for escaping the hardship caused by competition: sell the Nagapattinam factory to Tata itself.

That was all the provincial Premier could say. Under the present system, the government operating in Dravida Nadu cannot protect Dravidian industrial growth. Dravida Nadu must remain a northern hunting ground, and the Indian government must continue supporting that condition. This is an unavoidable consequence of remaining joined to the North.

Although iron ore occurs in several parts of the Madras Presidency, American experts recently investigated the ore at Sandur in Bellary district and found it high-grade. It is now established that Sandur and Salem contain deposits suitable for founding an iron and steel plant in the Presidency.

Replying in the Madras Assembly to a member's question about beginning such a factory, the Minister for Industrial Development said in substance: “According to reports submitted by geological surveyors, high-grade iron ore is available in this Presidency, and adequate limestone and water required by a factory are available near the deposits. The government therefore held the view that an ironworks could be established in Madras.”

Sir M. Visvesvaraya, honoured as an industrial expert and a major contributor to the creation of the Bhadravati Iron and Steel Works during his time in Mysore, likewise stated in the press that an excellent iron and steel plant could be established in the Madras Presidency with electric power.

If sufficient coal begins to be mined from the Cuddalore-Vriddhachalam belt, it too can power the works. Yet despite these facilities and expert testimony, the central government plans ironworks in the United Provinces and Orissa. It chooses to neglect Madras despite all its advantages.

One may suspect that this decision arose at the prompting of northern capitalists. Perhaps because a legislative member shared that suspicion, he asked in the Madras Assembly whether capitalists had conspired to exclude Madras.

Dravida Nadu pays substantial taxes to the central government, but factories created at public expense are established in the North. Though resources and facilities exist, there is no means because opportunity is denied. Dravida Nadu therefore declines without industrial growth.

If industrially backward Dravida Nadu is to develop, it requires industrial protection. Manufactured goods from another country must be prevented from entering and destroying growing Dravidian industries. This is possible only when full authority to restrict imports and develop domestic industry lies in Dravidian hands. Today that power belongs to the Indian government, so Dravida Nadu cannot protect itself.

To save itself, Dravida Nadu must free itself from its northern connection and begin governing independently.

It must be able to prohibit foreign goods that compete with products made here, impose duties ensuring imported goods cannot undersell local products, protect domestic industry, and export abundant local production to other countries according to its own choice. Only with such protective authority can all this be accomplished and resource-rich Dravida Nadu become a land overflowing with wealth.

Discussing what must be considered before establishing an ironworks in the Madras Presidency, the Madras minister named production cost, competition from industrial rulers outside and the availability of inexpensive electricity. Even the minister fears that external competition could crush provincial industrial growth. If that fear is to leave his mind and the minds of others, there is no path except a separate Dravida Nadu.

When the entire Indian subcontinent is treated as a single market and one private capitalist gains the right to manufacture or sell a particular commodity, he grows so powerful that nobody can compete with him. Even the prices of goods entering the subcontinent from abroad become whatever that capitalist determines.

To end this condition, the Indian government would have to assume full responsibility. It shows no sign of doing so, and there is no reason to expect it. If India were divided economically into several regional spheres, this single capitalist tyranny could not prevail.

For Dravida Nadu, there is certainly a way to remove it. Resources exist here, but there is no means. The sole remedy for obtaining that means is: “Dravida Nadu for Dravidians.”

8 May 1949.

---NOTES---
Images 2 and 3 in the supplied source repeat the same mineral-resource passage. The Tamil source is retained page-for-page; the English translates that duplicated passage once.

The Bellary place name appears in OCR as `சாந்தார்`, `சாந்தூர்`, and `சாத்தூர்`; it is rendered provisionally as Sandur from the geographic context. Formal place indexing should be checked against the scans and contemporary geological records.

Several historical mineral quantities and the damaged lines concerning export and protective tariffs are translated conservatively from context. They should not be treated as modern geological or economic data without independent verification.
